Drop the two onboarding screenshots, keep their instructions.

AllowVPN.png and InstallNetworkExtension.png are screenshots of macOS system
dialogs taken on a machine running the upstream client. They show "Pangolin.app",
the bundle identifier net.pangolin.Pangolin.PacketTunnel and the upstream app
icon — rendered as pixels, inside the image.

No substitution can reach that. brand/rules.sed rewrites text; the brand audit
greps text; both would pass while the onboarding flow shows a user another
product's name in two full-width images. This is the one brand surface in the
tree that tooling cannot see, which is exactly why it is called out here rather
than left to be noticed later.

Only the Image views are removed. The instructions above them are prose, sed
rewrites them correctly, and they are the part that actually helps someone
through the system-extension approval — the screenshots were illustration. The
imagesets themselves are deleted from the staging tree by scripts/rebrand.sh, so
the pixels do not travel into Assets.car either.

To restore them: take the two screenshots on a machine running a signed ZTARC
build, drop them into brand/assets/, and revert this patch. That cannot happen
until the app is signed, because the dialogs only appear when the system
